//? Title: Glow: Explicit Calibration and Signed Invertible Mixing
//? Subtitle: Two-channel ActNorm, actual Jacobians and the original banana-density run
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An invertible density model must evaluate a normalized likelihood and generate
observations from a simple latent distribution. Learned normalization and
coordinate mixing let successive affine couplings operate on different views
of those observations. This native C++ study checks explicit ActNorm calibration
and a negative-determinant mixing matrix before fitting the original compact
Glow model. Its complete program preserves the once-only first-32 fitting-row
calibration, all 48 Adam writes, both actual Jacobians and all 1,024 inverse
samples. The fixed-final test NLL is 2.866107941 nats per two-coordinate row,
better than the fitting-only full Gaussian's 2.994631823, while analytic truth
scores 2.796480843. This is a reproducible two-coordinate CPU teaching model,
not the full multiscale image architecture or a tuned benchmark result.
)
\toc

> Compose normalization, mixing and affine coupling in an invertible step

[Kingma and Dhariwal, Glow: Generative Flow with Invertible 1x1 Convolutions
(2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.03039) uses ActNorm, learned invertible mixing
and affine couplings. The source adapts four steps to two coordinates. ActNorm
is y=(x+b)exp(a) with a scalar log determinant sum(a). Row-vector mixing is
u=yW for a learned 2×2 matrix, contributing log|det W|. A coupling preserves
coordinate A and changes the other to u_B exp(s(u_A))+t(u_A), contributing
s(u_A). The conditioner is 1->16 tanh->2, with bounded **s=.8tanh(raw)**.
Alternate the conditioning coordinate by stage index modulo two.

For four stages, the exact change-of-variables calculation is

$$ \log p_X(x)=-\log(2\pi)-\tfrac12\|z\|^2+
\sum_{i=0}^3\left[\sum_c a_{ic}+\log|\det W_i|+s_i(u_{i,A})\right]. $$
\alttext(The observation log density is the standard Gaussian latent density plus each stage's ActNorm, mixing-matrix and per-point affine-coupling log determinants.)

The mean negative log density is measured in nats per two-dimensional row.
There is **no extra final global scale** in this source. Sampling supplies z
from the Gaussian base, then reverses coupling, mixing and ActNorm in stages
3,2,1,0. Recompute each conditioner on its own inverse path. The trained run
has positive actual Jacobian determinants; the mixing primitive also supports
negative signs, checked explicitly below. In either case likelihood uses a
log **absolute** determinant, never log of a negative number.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glow_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=32;
 d.node("norm",{-24,0},"Explicit ActNorm\n(x+b) exp(a)",s)
 .node("mix",{-11,0},"Signed 2x2 mixing\nRow vector y W",s)
 .node("couple",{2,0},"Bounded affine\nKeep one coordinate",s)
 .node("repeat",{15,0},"Repeat 4 steps\nReverse all to sample",s)
 .node("density",{28,0},"Gaussian base\nAdd all log |det|",s)
 .edge("norm","mix").edge("mix","couple").edge("couple","repeat").edge("repeat","density")
 .save_svg("build/glow_method.svg");
}
```
