// result_end

\caption(Every actual forward and inverse determinant is positive. The source maximum forward logdet error is 4.7514e-7; all inverse errors also satisfy 5e-6. Pool indices have separate meanings, so this overlay does not pair corresponding points.)
\alttext(128 blue forward and 1024 orange inverse logdet residuals cluster near zero at the displayed 1e7 scale, with no omitted rows.)

> Check normalization identities and both floating roundtrips

For samples x=f⁻¹(z), log p_X(x)+log|det Df⁻¹(z)| must equal log p_Z(z).
Every one of the 1,024 generated rows is checked, independently of the density
window. This is a change-of-variables consistency check, not a finite-window
quadrature approximation. The source tolerance is 2e-5 nats; its model base
constant is rounded as described above. The following native table also retains
the maximum errors of both floating roundtrips. Inversion has an exact algebraic
form, while Float32 operations still incur rounding error.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glow_normalization.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;std::map<std::string,n::Tensor> v;
 for(auto& [k,x]:n::load_npz("build/glow_density/plot_values.npz"))v[k]=x;
 auto error=v.at("normalization_error");
 dm::graphics::RPlot::create().points(n::arange(0,1024).to_vector<double>(),(error*1e6).to_vector<double>(),"All prior rows",{37,99,235},2)
 .y_limits(0,5).x_label("Original prior row index").y_label("Absolute identity residual x 1e6, nats")
 .title("Every inverse sample satisfies change of variables")
 .save_svg("build/glow_normalization.svg");
 std::print("| Exact-form / Float32 check | Maximum absolute error |\n|---|---:|\n| inverse(forward(test)) |{:.12g}|\n| forward(inverse(prior)) |{:.12g}|\n| Change-of-variables log identity |{:.12g}|\n",n::max(v.at("forward_roundtrip_absolute_error")).item(),n::max(v.at("inverse_roundtrip_absolute_error")).item(),n::max(error).item());
}
```
