|---|---:|
| inverse(forward(test)) |7.15255737305e-07|
| forward(inverse(prior)) |8.94069671631e-07|
| Change-of-variables log identity |2.10948697354e-06|
\image(figures/glow_normalization.svg)
// result_end

\caption(All 1,024 normalization residuals are plotted in original prior-row order, without subsampling. The checks also include bit-exact restore, repeated samples/Jacobians, single/batch parity, per-point determinant cancellation and immutable inference.)
\alttext(1024 blue absolute normalization residuals remain small at the displayed million-fold scale; no row exceeds the original 2e-5-nat tolerance.)

> What the checks establish

All original procedural pools, once-only sequential calibration, learned states/gradients, 48 Adam writes,
validation records, stage scale/shift fields, both actual Jacobians, every prior
sample, density grid, fitting-only moments and non-timing scalar metrics match
the source exactly. The independent NumPy audit composes ActNorm, mixing and analytic affine
Jacobian matrices, checks all 28 parameter leaves and four signed-fixture entries by finite differences,
verifies every update and inverse sample, and reconstructs all raw midpoint-MT
Box-Muller banks. It checks spatially varying determinants on **both** paths
without calling native model math or refitting a substitute.

This four-stage two-dimensional model demonstrates tractable per-point volume
change and exact-form inversion. Its original fixed-final likelihood improves
on fitted Gaussian baselines, while analytic truth remains better. That result
is specific to the retained 296-value architecture, seeds, tiny procedural pools
and predefined final epoch. The study does not claim full multiscale image Glow
performance, a tuned best model or uncertainty quantified across training runs.

